module

public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.LisseEtaleClosure

/-!
# The two topologies of the lisse-étale site agree (Stacks 0787, 0788)

`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/LisseEtaleSite.lean` gives the lisse-étale site of an
algebraic stack `𝒳` the topology `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.topology` generated by the
*chosen* étale covering families of the ambient site between smooth objects, while
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/LisseEtaleInclusion.lean` works with
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.inclusionTopology`, in which a family covers exactly when its
image covers in `𝒳_{étale}`. That file proves one inequality,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.topology_le_inclusionTopology`, and records that cocontinuity
of the inclusion does not transport across it.

This file proves the other inequality, so the two topologies are **equal** and every statement of
Stacks 0788 holds for either of them.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.inclusionTopology_le_topology'` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.topology_eq_inclusionTopology`: the comparison.

Continuity and cocontinuity of the inclusion of the smooth objects, for both topologies and with
no hypothesis, are `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.isContinuous_inclusion'`,
`isCocontinuous_inclusion'`, `isContinuous_inclusion_topology'` and
`isCocontinuous_inclusion_topology'` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.LisseEtaleSmoothClosure`, where Stacks 0788 part (1) is
registered.

## Implementation notes

What has to be produced, given a family `R` of morphisms of smooth objects whose image generates
a covering sieve of `𝒳_{étale}`, is a *chosen* étale covering family of smooth objects contained
in the sieve `R` generates; the inequality is then `superset_covering`. Both topologies in sight
are generated by a precoverage, and each of the three generating precoverages — the chosen étale
families of `B.Schemes`, the families inherited by `𝒳` from the localization at `S`, and the
families inherited by the full subcategory — has isomorphisms and is stable under composition and
base change, so Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.Precoverage.mem_toGrothendieck_iff_of_isStableUnderComposition` turns each
covering sieve into a covering family contained in it. Applying it twice, and then
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_etaleCovering_le_of_mem_etaleTopology` at the bottom, produces a chosen
étale family `E₂` of `B.Schemes` refining the image of `R`.

That family is then lifted back up, one member at a time. A member `E₂.f i` factors through the
image of a member `ρ` of the family inherited by `𝒳`, and the factor is lifted cartesianly
(`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFibered.exists_isStronglyCartesian`) to a morphism `v` of `𝒳` whose
source lies over `E₂.X i` **on the nose**, so that the lifted family pushes down to a family
combinatorially equivalent to `E₂` — and `AlgebraicGeometry.etaleCovering` depends only on the
presieve a family generates together with the cardinality of its index type, both of which
combinatorial equivalence preserves. The source of the lift is a smooth object because `E₂.f i`
is étale, hence smooth, and the smooth objects are closed under smooth morphisms; and the lift
lies in the sieve generated by `R` because the inclusion of a full subcategory is fully faithful,
so the factorization of `ρ` through `R` is the image of a factorization upstairs.

This is the step the source takes silently when it says the lisse-étale site is "the category of
schemes smooth over the stack with the étale topology": the étale topology of the ambient big
site is generated by a *chosen* system of families, and refining an arbitrary covering down to
that system is what makes the two descriptions of the covering sieves agree.

